WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.580
Five digit bounties seemed to me like&nbsp;
something reserved for top hunters with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:13.680
their crazy payloads. But recently, I made it.&nbsp;
I got a $20,000 bounty for a path traversal that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.560
allowed me to leak files of other users. In&nbsp;
this video, I will show you what the bug was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:26.400
and also why I think others have missed it so&nbsp;
make sure you stay until the end. But for now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:33.480
enjoy the explanation. I was short after listening&nbsp;
to a critical thinking episode with Alex Chapman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:40.920
and my understanding of his methodology is that he&nbsp;
goes mostly for high and critical vulnerabilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:47.880
But the thing is he goes for bugs that rely on&nbsp;
specific functionality. Because for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:53.880
when you have an IDOR, an IDOR can be in almost&nbsp;
every single request or authorization bug.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.600 --> 00:01:01.260
But for a bug like SSRF, you need a specific&nbsp;
functionality. And my understanding of Alex's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:07.920
methodology was that he goes for this&nbsp;
functionality. And I started looking at my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:14.880
unnamed target because it's a private bug bounty&nbsp;
program with this approach in mind. So I wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:22.380
find some functionality where I can find high and&nbsp;
critical risk bugs. But really, there wasn't much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:28.680
There wasn't much interesting functionality. But&nbsp;
one that caught my attention was uploading files&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:37.200
because I could upload an avatar to my profile.&nbsp;
I saw that this target uses Amazon S3 buckets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:43.920
and that they use the same bucket for avatars&nbsp;
and for other sensitive files that I as user can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:50.580
upload. So I knew that any bug here will be very&nbsp;
impactful. And when it comes to the integration&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:58.200
with Amazon S3, there are a few options. One,&nbsp;
the conventional option would be that you send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:04.980
a request to the server with your new profile&nbsp;
picture, and then the server talks with S3 sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:11.880
in the backend, and then your new avatar is saved.&nbsp;
And maybe the user does not even see that the S3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:19.740
is used. The problem with this approach is that it&nbsp;
is very consuming on the server because the server&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:27.600
sort of acts as a proxy between the S3 and the&nbsp;
user and its images, its other files, which are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:36.060
quite heavy. So there is another way to do this&nbsp;
functionality. The ideal method would be to just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:43.620
let the user talk directly with the S3 bucket,&nbsp;
bypassing the server, bypassing the need of server&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:53.460
to proxy the images. But then the problem you come&nbsp;
up to is how do you maintain an authorization when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.460 --> 00:03:00.660
the user talks directly to the bucket? Because&nbsp;
then I could just upload my avatars as avatar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.880
of another user. I could maybe delete them.&nbsp;
I could at least read other people's files.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:13.440
So this wouldn't be that good. That's why there&nbsp;
is the mechanism that's called pre-signed URLs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:20.940
And the way it works is I send the request to the&nbsp;
server, to my target, saying, hello, I'm Greg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:28.080
and I want to upload an avatar. I'm already&nbsp;
authenticated, so there is no problem with this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:36.480
And now the server responds with a URL, which&nbsp;
allows me to upload the file there. This URL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:45.000
allows me to talk to the S3 API, and I can only&nbsp;
upload my file to a specified location. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.080
URL is signed cryptographically, so I cannot&nbsp;
mess up with these parameters. And then after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:04:00.000
I send the request to the S3 API, I save my file&nbsp;
there. I confirm to my target server that, yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.820
I have saved my file at this location which&nbsp;
you gave me. So this is a three-step process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:14.160
and it was robust. I couldn't do anything. I tried&nbsp;
uploading XSS files. I tried path traversals. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:21.120
just nothing you can do here. When it comes to&nbsp;
this pre-signed URL, if you could break the SHA-1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:28.440
signature, you could probably break half of the&nbsp;
internet. So this is also not happening. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:34.440
three-step process is only uploading of the file,&nbsp;
and of course, the file has to be read somewhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:42.600
That's why we upload it. So then when I go to&nbsp;
my account page, I see that the image tag with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:51.300
my avatar, it has the pre-signed URL as well.&nbsp;
The difference is that this time, I didn't send&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:57.420
this request specifically for the pre-signed&nbsp;
URL. This time, the URL was just embedded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:03.780
the HTML of the response, and it contained&nbsp;
the link to my avatar, which I could request.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:14.880
And the file name that was used here is the name&nbsp;
I used in step three previously. So my idea was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:22.920
what if I send here a file that does not belong&nbsp;
to me? And the first thing that I tried was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:30.060
avatar that I've uploaded from my other account,&nbsp;
and it worked. And I could see on my account page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:38.940
the avatar uploaded from another account, which&nbsp;
didn't have any impact yet, but it showed me that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:47.880
there's probably no authorization check in place.&nbsp;
Then I moved one step ahead, and I, instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:56.220
using the avatar, I used another document uploaded&nbsp;
from my another account, something I just uploaded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:03.600
in another functionality of this application. And&nbsp;
when I went to the account page, my image did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:10.680
load. My image did not load, but the image tag&nbsp;
still had the pre-signed URL. And when I opened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:17.280
it in the new tab, it did show me the document,&nbsp;
the sensitive document from my another account.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:25.500
At this point, I was confident that this is&nbsp;
a valid bug and there is impact to it. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:32.340
the problem was that the name of the file from my&nbsp;
another account, I couldn't brute force it. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:38.820
too complex to be brute forcible. So I wanted&nbsp;
to find a way to leak this to get the maximum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:46.800
payout. I think if I reported the bug as it was,&nbsp;
I would get a payout, but probably as a P2 or P3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:55.500
and I wanted the maximum bounty. The thing with&nbsp;
pre-signed URLs is that they can point to a file,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:03.780
but they can also point to a directory. So what&nbsp;
I did was, instead of using the file name in step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:11.580
three, I just used the directory and I left it&nbsp;
this way. As always, I went to the account page,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.580 --> 00:07:18.480
my avatar didn't load, so I copied the pre-signed&nbsp;
URL from the image tag, pasted into the browser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:26.640
and I saw a lot of different files, which I saw&nbsp;
did not belong to me. Now, this funny problem I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:32.040
had is even though there were thousand files,&nbsp;
because this is by default, the limit of AWS,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:38.040
I didn't access any of them because I saw&nbsp;
that this is probably customer information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:43.980
but by file names, they didn't look as&nbsp;
sensitive as some of the files in the system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:55.380
So I found, I later found the AWS S3 parameters&nbsp;
that allow me to show more than 1,000 objects on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.380 --> 00:08:03.600
the directory listing or the object listing to&nbsp;
be precise. And at this point, I just knew that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:10.560
this is a critical bug, and as expected, I got&nbsp;
the maximum payout. I was really happy with it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:16.980
especially that it came after quite a bad period&nbsp;
when it comes to bug bounty. More on this in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:23.220
